  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nToday-Saturday... Upper level high pressure will remain in place as \nan upper level trough across the eastern US will continue to slowly \nshift south and eastward through the period. At the surface, high \npressure axis north of the local area will shift southward into \ncentral Florida into Saturday. Locally, light south/southwest winds \nwill shift onshore in the afternoon with the formation of the east \ncoast sea breeze and slow push inland both this afternoon and \nSaturday. The east coast sea breeze is forecast to push farther \ninland today, with the sea breeze collision forecast across the \ninterior, generally east of Orlando Metro and I-95 late in the \nafternoon and early evening. The sea breeze collision will then \noccur a little closer to the coast, generally around the I-95 \ncorridor, in the afternoon on Saturday. Upper level shortwave energy \nwill aid in storm formation each day. \n\nScattered to numerous showers and storms are forecast to form each \nafternoon (PoP 40-70%), first forming along the sea breeze, with \nhighest coverage occurring along the sea breeze collision. Storm \nmotions will generally be southwesterly today, and more westerly on \nSaturday. Forecast soundings show a very moist profile, with PW \nvalues of 2.0-2.2\". The environment supports convection, with plenty \nof instability (MUCAPE of 1800-2300 J/kg), sufficient buoyancy \n(lifted index of -5 to -7), adequate shear (sfc-6 km shear of 10-15 \nkt), and sufficient downdraft potential (DCAPE 700-900 J/kg). \nHowever, temperatures aloft are rather warm (-5C to -6C at 500 mb). \nBecause of this, main storm hazards will be frequent lightning, \ngusty winds up to 45 mph, and torrential downpours. Quick amounts of \n2-4\" of rainfall  in a relatively short period of time in these slow-\nmoving storms will be possible, promoting minor/nuisance flooding in \nurban and poor drainage areas. The Weather Prediction Center (WPC) \nmaintains a Marginal (level 1 of 4) Risk for excessive rainfall for \nall of east central Florida through at least Saturday. \n\nTemperatures will be seasonable to slightly above normal for this \ntime of year, and humid through Saturday. Afternoon highs will be in \nthe low to mid 90s each day with peak heat indices of 100-107F. Warm \nand muggy conditions will continue overnight, with lows in the low \nto mid 70s. \n\nSunday-Thursday... Upper level high pressure across the Great Plains \nwill shift eastward through mid next week as the trough over the \neastern US shifts eastward and offshore through early next week. At \nthe surface, and weak frontal boundary will drop into the Deep south \nand northern Florida by early next week, reaching central and south \nFlorida early to mid next week. This will result in deep moisture \nshifting over the area into at least Tuesday. Drier air will begin \nto filter across the area mid-late week. Because of this, higher \ncoverage of showers and storms are forecast, with PoPs forecast to be \n60-70% areawide on Sunday, 80% areawide Monday, 60-80% Tuesday, and \n40-70% Wednesday and Thursday. Storm motion will generally be light \nand offshore-moving. Storm hazards will be similar to what they are \ntoday. WPC outlooked all of east central Florida in a Marginal \n(level 1 of 4) Risk of Excessive Rainfall on Sunday, and at least \nportions of east central Florida on Monday. Seasonable temperatures \nand humid conditions are forecast each day, with highs in the upper \n80s to low 90s. Warm and muggy conditions will continue overnight, \nwith lows in the low to mid 70s.",
